00:06:45.159 --> 00:06:45.700
risk manager.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:52.690
So let's start with, what
is credit risk management?

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.270
To answer that question,
I'll actually go back

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.190
to the basics of banking.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.390
If you looked at banks,
banks are a place

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:04.770
where you go to to deposit
your money, to borrow money,

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:09.060
or to transfer money from
one place to the other end.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.650
In essence, we're just a
financial services company.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:17.310
So if you as an individual
deposit your money with a bank,

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.580
the bank will actually
lend that money

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.710
to a client who is
in need of money.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.030
And when we do that,
we actually run a risk,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.610
and that risk is
called credit risk.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:34.284
It's the risk that when we
lend out money to a client,

00:07:34.284 --> 00:07:35.950
we run the risk that
the client will not

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:37.610
be able to repay that money.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:41.440
In other words, they
won't be able to adhere

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.940
to the obligation
towards the bank.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.310
If you look very
closely to this picture,

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:52.470
you will actually see that
you as a private individual

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.570
are also running a risk,
but then on the bank.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:59.320
And as a result of that,
we are a regulated entity.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.520
So we need to abide to a lot
of rules and regulations just

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.860
to make sure that your
money is safe with us.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:09.870
And within this whole scheme,
having proper early warning

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.200
systems are quite important.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.070
So now, let's look at what
happens when we actually

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.660
lend out money.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.970
What we'll do to
assess the risk,

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.020
we'll do a lot of analysis.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.100
We'll look at the financial
health of a company.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.184
We'll look at the management
team of the company,

00:08:27.184 --> 00:08:28.850
whether they are able
to actually adhere

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:30.474
to the strategies
that they've set out.

00:08:30.474 --> 00:08:32.730
We'll look at macroeconomic
type of events.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.929
We'll look at the balance
sheet, the net profitability

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.039
of the company, and so forth.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.669
And what we'll do
is actually, we

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:42.890
input all these
variables into what

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:44.840
we call a credit risk model.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.330
These are models that
we've internally developed,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.730
which have also been validated
by independent parties

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:53.810
within the bank and also
[INAUDIBLE] by the regulator.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:55.680
So when we input
all these variables,

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:57.140
we get a risk rating.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.770
And a risk rating is nothing
more than an output of a model.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:04.260
And it gives you the
classification of the risk.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.880
So with ING, we
use a number code--

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.740
one being very good risk,
whilst 18 being very bad risk.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.740
External rating agencies
actually do the same thing.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.920
They used AAA for very low
risk, whilst BBB and beyond

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.640
is very high risk.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.081
So when we've
engaged with a client

00:09:24.081 --> 00:09:25.830
and we've determined,
actually, that we're

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:28.020
willing to run the risk
because it's within our risk

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.450
appetite, what we'll
do is we'll start

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.260
monitoring that client, which
means that we're actually

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:39.240
starting to see whether there
are any signals out there which

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.810
actually causes any potential
issues that we would have

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.820
with the client going forward.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.504
This is what we call
Early Warning Signals.

00:09:48.504 --> 00:09:50.170
And then on a daily
basis, what we'll do

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:54.460
is we'll use sources
like news, market prices,

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.130
but also like interim figures.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:59.380
I could give you a lot of
examples of early warning

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.000
signals, but these are quite
different per type of client

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.100
and per type of industry.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:07.300
So what I'll do is I'll just run
you through the day of a risk

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.550
manager.

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:12.250
So let's meet Charles.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.009
Actually, he's
almost the same guy

00:10:14.009 --> 00:10:15.550
as in the introduction
movie but then

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:18.490
with a different hair color.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.040
Well, Charles is a
credit risk manager

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.890
who has a portfolio of 100
clients, which are globally

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:25.340
active.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.460
He has been with the bank
for about like 10 years.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:30.070
He's a very seasoned banker.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:32.352
So he actually has
developed a sixth sense

00:10:32.352 --> 00:10:34.810
which actually tells him when
he's looking at a transaction

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:37.180
whether it's a good or bad risk.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.030
And in line with all credit
risk managers who are out there,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:44.580
we don't like any
type of surprises.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.560
So let's look at Charles's day.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.750
First of all, he
gets into the office.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.980
He grabs a cup of coffee and
then goes through his paces.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.950
Basically, he looks
at the risks--

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:59.100
I mean the news, which is
relevant for his portfolio.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.470
And then, he also runs
through all the transactions,

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:04.320
so he looks at transactions
that he needs to approve

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.910
or whether he will decline them.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.950
He looks at annual reviews.

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:12.330
And he also looks
at rating models.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.400
So Charles has quite a busy day.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.650
And in that day, he
also needs to make sure

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.440
that he captures all the signals
and information out there

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.410
for his 100 clients.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:24.470
Now, let's imagine
the following.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.574
One day, Charles is actually
reading the newspaper,

00:11:27.574 --> 00:11:28.365
a global newspaper.

00:11:28.365 --> 00:11:30.120
And he finds out that
one of his client

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:37.360
is involved in a bribery case in
a very specific country, which

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.030
surprises him, because he
didn't know about them.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.330
So being a risk manager, he
starts digging in deeper.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.580
And then he finds out that,
actually, this information

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.830
was already available
a month ago but only

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:52.610
in that very specific country
and the very specific language.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.005
Digging deeper, he
actually finds out

00:11:55.005 --> 00:11:56.630
that the stock price
has been declining

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:00.836
for like a month for 10%, and
he didn't know about that.

00:12:00.836 --> 00:12:02.210
As you can imagine,
Charles being

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:07.790
a person who isn't fond of
surprises, he is quite upset.

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:09.340
Now, let's look at
another example.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:10.400
Meet Lauren.

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:14.300
Lauren is also a credit risk
manager but then for banks

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.324
and for countries.

00:12:15.324 --> 00:12:17.240
And her portfolio is
actually much more geared

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:19.976
toward market-based
type of prices.

00:12:19.976 --> 00:12:21.600
OK, thanks, I hope
you can hear me now.

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:27.140
Well, actually what it
means is that Lauren

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:32.180
has to look whether the risk
rating profile of that client

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:33.822
needs to be just
adjusted or not.

00:12:36.759 --> 00:12:38.550
Well, actually, she
finds out that the news

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.150
was already available
a day ago when

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:44.530
the external rating agencies
downgraded the country.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.650
Like Charles, she's
not really happy.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:50.720
So what do Lauren and
Charles have in common?

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:55.360
Well, basically, they have to
deal with a lot of information

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.700
whilst they have to do
their regular day job.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.150
Imagine that you have to
look-- you have like 2,000

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:06.750
counterparties across the
globe in 75 languages with more

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.570
than 20,000 sources that you
need-- that you get information

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:10.350
from.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:12.030
That's a lot.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:17.350
So they run the risk of losing
out on early warning signals.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:21.970
So we identified this problem
within the wholesale risk

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:26.080
management space, and we wanted
to have a tool which actually

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.090
gives us information
with big coverage

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:35.550
in news market data, which is
relevant for the risk manager.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:41.520
So we can actually move faster
and catching these signals,

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:45.181
and be more proactive as a
risk management department

00:13:45.181 --> 00:13:47.430
and moving toward what we
call a continuous monitoring

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:48.990
framework.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:55.840
So the tool that has been built
now is actually a first step.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:59.290
It has global and
local news sentiment.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:04.840
It has market prices, such as
equity, CDS, commodity prices,

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:10.690
and for countries,
organizations and sectors.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.040
Users can actually set
their own threshold levels.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.870
And when something
is reached, the tool

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:21.160
will actually signal or give
a notification to the user.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:25.570
For us, this is a first
step, because we always

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.090
anticipated when we
started with this project

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:32.920
that we want to have a tool
which is much more predictive.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:36.890
Remember the sixth sense that
I talked about with Charles?

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:38.969
Well, Charles,
this sixth sense is

00:14:38.969 --> 00:14:40.510
nothing more than
a heuristic that he

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:43.010
developed across the years.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:45.820
And we believe that with
the current data which

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:49.806
is available, but also with
the existing and future machine

00:14:49.806 --> 00:14:51.430
learning technology
which is out there,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:56.460
we can make a tool that
is much more predictive.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.530
So why did we build
it from scratch?

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:03.890
Well we, as ING,
we're a data company.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.300
And we think that it
has a lot of advantages

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.270
to build these types of
applications yourself.

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:15.590
First of all, in today's
world, it's relatively easy in

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.390
and you can build these
tools quite quickly

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.790
for a very specific use case.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:25.150
Second of all, it gives
you a lot of flexibility.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:28.090
It makes you also independent
from other solution providers,

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:30.240
which actually provide
a lot of features

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.460
that you often don't need.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:33.910
Besides the fact
that we, as ING,

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:35.960
want to be masters
of our own destiny,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:40.200
we've actually proven that we
can build these type of tools

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.827
ourselves.

00:15:41.827 --> 00:15:43.410
So that's why we
actually partnered up

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:48.690
with Google and PwC
to build this tool.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.910
We're at the stage
now that we're--

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:55.890
we have tested the tool across
eight countries with 30 users.

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:57.750
And we're now determining
the next steps

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:01.530
in developing the tool
further but, more importantly,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.830
to embed it in our core
monitoring processes going

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:06.670
forward.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.580
So I hope now you get
a better feeling why

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.540
we need to have the tool
but also that credit risk

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:17.130
management is a very
data-driven and also

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:21.240
a very analytical
function within the bank.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.200
Well, with that, I want to
thank you for your attention.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:26.290
Sorry about that.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:27.045
Thank you for your attention.

00:16:27.045 --> 00:16:28.320
And I'll hand it back to Otto.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:31.603
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:34.295 --> 00:16:35.670
OTTO VERMEULEN:
Thank you, Anand.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:37.380
That was very clear.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:39.360
I think we now know
what the bank does

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:42.990
and that credit risk management
is extremely important to them.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.540
Also, good to hear that
the proof of concept

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:47.220
addressed your
early warning signal

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:50.190
challenges and also that
you are looking forward

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:54.640
to use machine learning to
get more predictive signals.

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:58.620
So now, I think it's time
that we hear about project has

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:01.350
evolved and what the
design principles were,

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.250
so over to Pieter Verheijen.

00:17:07.642 --> 00:17:10.510
PIETER VERHEIJEN: Hey, everyone.

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:12.910
It's great to be
here and talk to you

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:15.490
about our proof of concept.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:18.609
Personally, I'm very
passionate about solving

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:22.819
important problems and making
innovation really happen.

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:26.000
And I will, in my part, run
you through the approach

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.580
that we applied to
this proof of concept;

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:31.580
secondly, the design of this
solution and the underlying

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.920
business needs; and
thirdly, I will give you

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:38.210
a demo of the solution itself.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.960
To start with the first
part, the approach-- for me,

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:44.380
there are three
crucial ingredients

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.540
that have been part
of this proof concept.

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:49.000
The first is the team.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.670
You need to have the
right skills and people

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.260
to deliver fast.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:57.380
In our case, we created
five skill sets--

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:02.120
an IT architect; a user
experience designer;

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:05.330
a user interface developer
to implement the design;

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:08.990
data scientists to build
models, to detect early warning

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:12.410
signals; and credit risk
expertise from Anand

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:16.900
and his team to tell us actually
what is relevant to see.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.080
My lesson learned from building
such a team is that you need

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:21.970
to find the right balance--

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:25.000
the balance of experts
who can deliver very fast

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:28.150
in their area of
expertise, but also skill

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.010
overlaps so that you
build, in the end,

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:33.270
a solution that fits together.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:36.800
The second ingredient
was the way of working.

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:40.220
We really were benefiting
from direct user feedback

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.270
all the time.

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:43.250
We applied an agile
way of working.

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:45.530
And we started with
a demo that was only

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:49.650
built in two weeks' time,
shown to the end users.

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:53.120
And based on that approach, we
got a very valuable feedback

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:54.860
and a dialogue that
you could build upon

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:56.780
for the rest of the project.

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:59.060
We took that feedback
into our backlog, started

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:01.490
planning the first print,
and from that moment

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:06.720
on, the rhythm was there that
we could work with the end-users

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.820
continuously.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:12.280
The third ingredient
was scoping.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:16.320
We only were you using
public data in the beginning.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.930
And that increased the
speed tremendously.

00:19:19.930 --> 00:19:22.680
We didn't need to connect
with any other existing

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:26.150
IT system within a bank
at that point in time,

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:29.063
and that was very
important for us.

00:19:29.063 --> 00:19:32.700
Now, the approach
that we applied

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.320
had three phases
on a high level.

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:37.440
The first phase was
really the beginning

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.540
when we needed to
start from scratch.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.600
Nothing there on the Google
Cloud Platform environment,

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.730
just the project name.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.790
Then, we started with
the three ingredients

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:50.070
that I just explained
and with the services

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.140
available in the
Google Cloud Platform

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:55.150
to develop the
solution in sprints.

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:57.150
And I think the
success factor was

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:01.310
that we had a successful
demo every sprint

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.160
and at the end of
six sprints a working

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:09.890
proof of concept ready for
user testing at that time.

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:13.820
In our case, this, for me,
really demonstrated the power

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:16.730
that you get from building proof
of concepts with the Google

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:18.800
Cloud, that you can really go

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.987
fast with your
proof of concepts.

00:20:21.987 --> 00:20:24.070
In our case, it was a
successful proof of concept,

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:25.290
so we decided to move on.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:28.830
And the second phase was
about testing it with users--

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:32.820
as Anand explained, eight
locations around 30 users.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.710
And through those
user pilots, we

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.570
gathered a lot of
feedback and data

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:40.590
that we could use to retrain
the models for machine learning

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.780
perspective and decide
what is actually

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:46.440
relevant to see for users.

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.390
And now, we are in
the third phase,

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:51.600
where we are deciding how to
roll this out further and take

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:56.890
this into production into the
core processes of the bank.

00:20:56.890 --> 00:21:00.990
Now, that was the approach on
how we did this proof concept.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:05.370
Now, quickly, I want to take you
through what we exactly built.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:11.310
This is a very simplistic
representation of our solution.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:14.190
On the bottom, you see
two types of data sources

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:16.240
that we ingest--

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.070
firstly, market data in terms
of equity prices, credit default

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.980
swaps, bonds, and other
financial instruments

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:24.510
coming from Thomson
Reuters based

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:27.070
on the license that
ING has with them;

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:34.680
secondly, news coming from
public sources via Google,

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.790
GDELT, and others.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:40.630
That's something we uploaded
into the Google Cloud Platform,

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:42.840
ingest it, and there we
have built a pipeline

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.007
to process and analyze
that information.

00:21:45.007 --> 00:21:47.340
And that middle layer is also
where the machine learning

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:48.540
is happening.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.520
And then based on
that pipeline, we

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.910
decide what to
display to a user who

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:57.540
is able to log into an
interface and from there,

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.780
get that information to him.

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.810
On the right side, you
see some statistics

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:06.630
of what we are
actually processing.

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.410
The current scope is around
250 clients, or organizations,

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:10.830
in the tool.

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:13.800
And you can get an impression
of the amount of information

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:15.510
that we are processing
on a daily basis.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:22.120
Now, that was a very
simplistic picture.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:25.000
And actually,
through those prints,

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:27.850
we encountered some
very specific business

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:30.940
needs that we needed to
fulfill and overcome to build

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.820
a successful proof of concept.

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.240
Five are here on the street.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.560
You need to have maximum
coverage delivered as signals

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.760
in real time with
enriched information

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:45.560
so that you are sure that
their imputation is right,

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:48.170
filtered and ranked
based on relevance,

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:51.400
and then, in the end,
clustered into single events.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:53.470
And I will go through
each of those in more

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.950
detail in the coming slides.

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:59.230
The first part is coverage.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:03.040
We really learned that entity
profiles drive the coverage

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:05.460
that we were looking for.

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:09.390
In one of the first
demos, we actually

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.880
got the feedback that
we were missing out

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:13.580
on important information.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:15.810
And as Anand explained,
you don't want

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.460
to miss out on those signals.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.710
The reason for missing
out on information

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:23.980
was that we were only looking
for information with the parent

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:26.105
company name.

00:23:26.105 --> 00:23:27.730
And actually, we
learned that we should

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:28.880
take a different approach.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:31.930
We should build entity
profiles with all the aliases,

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:36.580
subsidiaries underneath that
company in different languages,

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.310
as you can see here
as an example for ING.

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:43.210
And in that way, we have been
able to increase the coverage

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:47.760
with a factor of five to 10,
on average, for each client.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:51.300
Now, the information
is then coming in,

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.359
and you want to process
that in real time.

00:23:53.359 --> 00:23:55.150
That's a key requirement
for risk managers,

00:23:55.150 --> 00:23:58.890
so that he gets those signals
fast and can respond to them.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.290
As you see here,
for an average risk

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:03.186
manager with a
portfolio of 50 clients,

00:24:03.186 --> 00:24:04.560
that's quite a
lot of information

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.240
that we need to handle.

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.900
On a daily basis, you talk
about 15,000 news items,

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:13.810
250 financial instruments
which are continuously moving,

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.320
and that's all
something we need to be

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.190
able to process in real time.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.370
So how can we help
the risk manager

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.630
to stay on top of these things?

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:24.930
In the technical
part done by Peter,

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:26.940
we will talk about the
architecture and the way

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:28.840
we developed a
solution to make this--

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:32.180
to meet this requirement.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:36.210
The third one is enrichment.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:38.860
So as a risk
manager, you actually

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:44.050
want to have information that
is truly about the organization

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:46.360
that you are responsible for.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:50.340
So you need to be able to
cut out the false positives.

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:55.170
As the example here shows you,
if you get a average news item,

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:58.620
you see a lot of actors
mentioned in the news item.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:02.490
So if you would do
a keyword search

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:06.260
for any of the organizations,
this item would whip up.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:09.470
But if you are looking
for early warning signals,

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:12.950
only in the case
of oil company E,

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:16.020
this is a true positive
early warning signal.

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:17.635
So the rest are
just false positive,

00:25:17.635 --> 00:25:19.420
and you want to
filter those out.

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:22.290
So we need to go beyond
the keyword method

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:28.030
and applying techniques to
understand who, what, when,

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:30.570
and where this event
actually is happening.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:34.360
And we use the natural language
processing technique for that,

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:37.780
and we will show later
on how that works.

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:40.870
The fourth part is now
we went very broad.

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:42.874
A lot of information
is coming in.

00:25:42.874 --> 00:25:43.790
We also enriched that.

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:47.350
So it's really about this
counterparty, or client.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.860
But now, there's still too
much information to handle.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:53.160
So you need to be able to
filter that information

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.190
and select items which are
actually relevant for a credit

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.970
risk manager to take a look at.

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:00.620
As you can see
here, there are also

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:02.930
some examples of things
you don't want to take

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:04.540
a look at as a risk manager.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:07.330
So if you are responsible
for monitoring a bank

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:10.400
and you're getting
the global news,

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.370
you also see a lot of local
news about ATM incidents,

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:14.490
for instance--

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.280
negative sentiment but not
relevant for a credit risk

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:18.830
manager on that level.

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:20.240
Same with car
manufacturers-- you

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:23.730
get in news about car
accidents as well.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:26.120
So what we need to
do is actually make

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:30.890
that distinction in our pipeline
to detect what type of topics

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.260
are relevant for
credit risk manager

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:37.040
to take look at and cut out
the other part, which is just

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:39.380
noise frame.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:40.460
And then the fourth--

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:43.260
or, sorry-- the fifth
one is the clustering.

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:47.070
So now, we have relevant
items to take a look at.

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:50.620
But as you can imagine,
if such an event happens,

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:52.570
more publishers are
writing about it.

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:55.517
And it becomes a very annoying
experience for a user,

00:26:55.517 --> 00:26:57.100
because you get that
same news message

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:00.010
over and over again but
then from a different news

00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:01.390
publisher.

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:06.310
So what you need to do to solve
this is build a clustering

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:11.110
algorithm that understands,
that actually based on the text,

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:13.210
this is talking
about the same event.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:15.490
And that's what we did,
so that you cluster it

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:18.650
into a single event and
bring that message only once

00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:20.210
to the user.

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:23.350
Now, these are the five
building blocks of our solution.

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:25.180
The last thing I
would like to do

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:27.700
is take you through a demo
of what the solution now

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.850
really looks like.

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.730
For the purpose of
the demo, we don't

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:35.460
want to harm anyone here
in the room with showing

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.060
real early warning signals.

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:39.600
So we created a
simulated dataset

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:43.110
with dummy names and
dummy news items.

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:46.627
The rest of the tool is
working just as it is.

00:27:46.627 --> 00:27:51.820
Now, Charles was logging in.

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:55.730
As you can see, nothing
there yet on his home page.

00:27:55.730 --> 00:27:58.460
So the first thing he does
is he goes to his portfolio,

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:00.670
and he wants to
add counterparties

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:03.460
to his organization-- a
list, but you can also

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:06.450
start searching for names.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:09.140
So that's what he's
doing right now.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:12.260
And he adds a
second organization

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:20.500
to his portfolio, third one,
and a fourth organization.

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:23.040
So now, he has four
organizations in his portfolio.

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:24.840
Yes, there they are.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:27.930
And then he will click top left
going back to the home page.

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:31.060
And on the left side of the
screen, you see the news feed.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:36.300
And the news feed has factors
like sentiment score, dates,

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:38.160
entities found.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:42.370
You can also filter
on the time period.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:44.970
You can filter the news
on various factors,

00:28:44.970 --> 00:28:48.210
like relevance or
sentiment negative

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:53.990
or popularity, which means
how much is written about it.

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:57.980
And then here, you see that
he goes to a specific article

00:28:57.980 --> 00:28:59.580
which has five similar items.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:00.950
That's the clustering.

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:05.400
So these are all about the same
event in different languages

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:06.350
with different titles.

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:10.530
So this is, I think,
Chinese, a French article--

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:13.980
FR is French, translated,
and then clustered that

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.782
into that single event.

00:29:15.782 --> 00:29:17.990
On the right side, you see
the market information, so

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:18.870
equity prices.

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:21.440
So the first one
is a negative one.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:22.580
So it went down.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:25.370
So he takes a look at this
specific counterparty page.

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:28.600
We had much more
detailed information--

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:34.490
equity price, credit default
swap, credit ratings.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:36.560
But actually, this
is all very reactive.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:39.520
You need to go and look
for the information.

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:42.460
But what you can do as well
is there-- set your triggers

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:43.810
and make it proactive.

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:49.530
So let's say the equity
price drops 5% in a day.

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:51.330
That's what he's doing now.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.190
He adds that to his portfolio.

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:57.920
He can do the same thing
with, for instance, keywords.

00:29:57.920 --> 00:29:59.670
In case of fraud, for
instance, if there's

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:01.170
a news item with
fraud in it, I want

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:03.120
to be notified immediately.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:04.690
And that's what the
trigger is doing.

00:30:04.690 --> 00:30:06.565
So it's already running
now in the background

00:30:06.565 --> 00:30:09.970
and checking if there are any
of those triggers being hit.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:14.070
New sentiment-- very
negative, another trigger.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:17.320
Credit ratings--
if there's probably

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:18.960
a downgrade, that's
something you

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:21.870
want to be notified
of immediately.

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:24.750
And in this way, it becomes
a very proactive way

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:28.140
of getting that information,
because now, those triggers

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:30.600
will search for you and make
sure that that information is

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:31.620
coming to you.

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:35.470
There's already one been
hit now around fraud.

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.500
So there has been an
article about fraud

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:40.140
on this specific client.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:40.640
Take a look.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:42.340
Yes, there it is.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:48.570
And now, sentiment score of mine
is 70, so it's relatively high.

00:30:48.570 --> 00:30:53.920
And now, the user is giving you
feedback on the specific item.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:55.720
I'd like to see more about that.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:58.780
Or for instance, the second one,
he doesn't consider relevant.

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:02.220
So that's information we
can use to actually make

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:05.760
those models that we have
running in the background more

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.920
advanced with machine learning.

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:10.890
So that was a very
short demo, but I

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:13.560
hope it demonstrated to
you that we have been able

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:17.340
to actually create a digital
assistant for a credit risk

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.240
manager to make his
job much more easy

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:25.620
with increased coverage,
increased response time,

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:29.970
and in the end, a proactive
tool that makes sure

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:32.640
that the information comes
to him at the right time.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:36.680
With that, I would like to
thank you and hand back to Otto.

