00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:28.250
I hope you are too.

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:33.850
So let's start with
a schematic overview.

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.710
So can we switch back,
please, to the slide?

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.250
Yeah, thank you.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:43.430
So what you see here
is a schematic overview

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:46.100
of the solution we
built into Google Cloud.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:49.040
As you can see, we use a
lot of managed Google Cloud

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:53.120
Services, which really
us to develop quickly.

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:58.287
On the left side, you see the
input we used for the pipeline

00:32:58.287 --> 00:32:59.370
you see on the right side.

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:03.530
So the input consists
of the entity profiler

00:33:03.530 --> 00:33:06.570
and consists of public
data sources to news.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:10.070
So on the right side, we
have the pipeline we built.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:12.350
And as you can see,
it consists mainly

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:14.970
of two very
important components.

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:18.530
One of them is Google Cloud
Pub/Sub, and one of them

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:22.150
is Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:27.610
This combination made sure that
this pipeline is really robust

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:29.350
but still very flexible.

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:31.540
And I will show you
why later in the demo.

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.520
This pipeline, it separates
into a couple of steps.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:42.750
And every step is its own
Kubernetes deployment.

00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:45.630
So we could easily
scale it independently

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:49.140
from the other
Kubernetes deployments.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.590
And all deployments
communicate via Pub/Sub.

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:55.740
So the input comes from
Pub/Sub, and the output

00:33:55.740 --> 00:34:00.430
goes to Pub/Sub until processing
and article is finished.

00:34:00.430 --> 00:34:05.460
So what I want to do now is
switch to the demo, please.

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:09.889
I want to take you on
a trip of an article

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:12.469
through the pipeline.

00:34:12.469 --> 00:34:15.230
And we'll do this in
a Jupyter notebook.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:18.949
I hope you can read
it from the back.

00:34:18.949 --> 00:34:22.350
And what we will do is we will
start with building an entity

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:23.949
profile.

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:27.380
In this case, we
will just use PwC.

00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:29.940
It's the only keyword
we use as an input.

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:34.320
And we will only build a part
of the entity profile, which

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:39.340
is gathering the aliases and
Wikipedia pages of this entity.

00:34:39.340 --> 00:34:41.270
So we will run this.

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:43.889
And as you can see, we
find quite some aliases,

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:47.830
some subsidiaries in
different languages,

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:51.510
different character
sets, different namings.

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:54.179
And we use all these
aliases to search

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:58.800
for news about this company.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:03.340
What you also see is a couple
of Wikipedia identifiers,

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:05.710
and these are really important
for us in a later stage.

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:14.410
So what we'll do now is we'll
use one of the aliases--

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:19.480
in this case, number
five, which is just PwC.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:24.085
And what we do is we
generate RSS feed URLs

00:35:24.085 --> 00:35:25.570
to the Google News.

00:35:25.570 --> 00:35:28.600
So as you can see, we
used the word as a query

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:30.440
for this RSS feed.

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:34.390
And what you also see is we
query for specific editions.

00:35:34.390 --> 00:35:37.030
And an edition on Google
News is a combination

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:40.420
of a language and a country.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:46.619
So in this case, it
would be a French article

00:35:46.619 --> 00:35:47.410
written in Belgium.

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:56.010
For this example, we will
query one Google News RSS feed,

00:35:56.010 --> 00:36:00.890
and we will only one
edition, the Dutch edition.

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:03.410
So what we will do
is we parse the feed.

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:04.640
We get the feed in.

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:07.850
And what you see here is
the first five articles

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:09.990
of this RSS feed.

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:13.610
It's in Dutch, so probably you
won't be able to understand it,

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.390
but that will come later.

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:20.470
What we get from the RSS
feed here is the headline,

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:23.390
is the publish date, and the
URL to the original article.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:31.750
So this is the first part
where Pub/Sub comes in.

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:36.430
Because before this,
we have to start

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:40.870
with a pipeline at a moment
where we start with the cure.

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:44.050
And then what cure does-- it was
located on the previous slide--

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:46.900
what a cure does
is it periodically

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:50.510
publishes a task to
Pub/Sub where it says, hey,

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:56.760
can you query on PwC on the
Dutch edition on Google News?

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.440
So that's done now.

00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:01.320
And what we now do is for every
article we get from this RSS

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:05.230
feed, we publish a task
to Pub/Sub saying, hey,

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:08.280
can you process
this article for me?

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:12.270
And we have different
workers which

00:37:12.270 --> 00:37:17.610
listen to this Pub/Sub topic,
get the task, and process it.

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:21.270
So it's as easy as this, just
I publish to a specific topic,

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:26.560
and I subscribe to a specific
subscription within that topic.

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:30.580
What I want to show you
now is some statistics,

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:34.250
so we will open this one.

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:37.190
What you see here is some
metrics from Pub/Sub.

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:40.430
In the first chart, you
see how many messages

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:42.380
we publish to Pub/Sub.

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:46.290
In this case, it's
about almost, I

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:50.230
think, 2,000 per
second at one point.

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:52.860
And we do it for, I think,
a couple of minutes, which

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:55.740
leads to a total queue
in Pub/Sub of articles

00:37:55.740 --> 00:38:01.390
to process of 200,000
articles can be duplicates.

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:03.910
So here, you see
the entire queue.

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:06.120
And what you here
see is the amount

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:09.810
of articles, the
worker's process.

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:13.800
So it's about, I
think, 30,000 per--

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.370
this is five minutes.

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:19.560
So in like 30 minutes, we
process the entire queue.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:21.240
And then we just wait for--

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:25.090
we search, again,
on those entities.

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:30.540
And this is a graph showing
the age of the oldest

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:35.180
task in this Pub/Sub queue, so
which is about 2,600 seconds.

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:36.240
I don't know, it's--

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:38.400
I think it's 40 minutes.

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:41.580
And what makes this
setup really nice

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:44.680
is that we can scale the
workers independently.

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:46.750
So let's say you think
this is too long.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:50.270
I don't want to wait 40
minutes if something happens.

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:51.770
I want to know it quicker.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:53.550
Then, we can say,
OK, let's scale

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:55.050
the workers independently.

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:58.230
And let's scale them to, for
example, the double amount

00:38:58.230 --> 00:38:58.960
of workers.

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:01.560
And then, this
number, this 30,000,

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:03.960
will increase to 60,000.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.790
And your queue will be
processed and half the time.

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:10.410
So I would like to
show how easy this is.

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:12.630
First, I'd like to
know, how many of you

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:17.110
have hands-on experience
with Kubernetes?

00:39:17.110 --> 00:39:17.870
OK, that's nice.

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:19.490
That's quite some.

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:23.270
You probably know how to
scale a Kubernetes deployment.

00:39:23.270 --> 00:39:24.950
For the people who
don't, I would like

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:27.230
to show you how easy it is.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:31.770
So let me zoom in a little.

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:35.960
And what we do here
is we see these

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:39.860
are the workers in process,
the first part of an article.

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:45.386
You see-- I think it's
12 containers or 12 bots.

00:39:45.386 --> 00:39:47.260
So what we want to do
is we want to scale it.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:51.440
So we are not happy with this
30-minutes processing time.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:53.680
So what we will do is--

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.080
let me type correctly there.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:58.870
So here, it says we
want to run 12 replicas.

00:39:58.870 --> 00:40:00.820
So let's say we change to 18.

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:05.440
So one's 50% more bots running.

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:08.010
Then, you immediately
see that now, Kubernetes

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:09.850
spawns up more containers.

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:12.040
And they are
running already now.

00:40:12.040 --> 00:40:13.930
And they listen to the
same Pub/Sub topic.

00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:20.110
So Pub/Sub makes sure
the tasks are distributed

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:22.040
over these containers.

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:23.920
So if we now would
wait for, I think,

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:28.660
a couple of minutes and the
pipeline would start again,

00:40:28.660 --> 00:40:32.990
this bar would be at like
45,000 per five minutes.

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:34.640
So it's that easy to scale this.

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:45.670
So let's assume this article
arrived at the processor.

00:40:45.670 --> 00:40:47.520
Then, we instantiate
a new article,

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:50.880
and we have the same information
that was in the RSS feed.

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:53.580
So the information
was sent to Pub/Sub,

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:57.380
and we get this information from
Pub/Sub, so nothing added here.

00:40:57.380 --> 00:40:58.230
Just a URL.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:01.020
We have the original headline
in the original language

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:03.770
and the publish date.

00:41:03.770 --> 00:41:06.950
What we do now is we
give the worker the task

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:10.232
to visit the website
site and collect the body

00:41:10.232 --> 00:41:10.940
from the website.

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:13.910
So what you here see is
the body of the article.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:16.860
It's in Dutch.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:21.530
And we need this for
further processing.

00:41:21.530 --> 00:41:23.030
What we do after
this is, of course,

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:24.080
we translate the article.

00:41:24.080 --> 00:41:27.860
Because we process, I think,
75 languages, and we all

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:29.030
need it in English.

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.100
So we use the Google
Cloud Translator

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:35.510
API to translate the headline
and the body of the article.

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:37.490
So now, you know what
this article is about.

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:41.026
It says, Ad van Gils is the
new CEO of PwC Netherlands.

00:41:41.026 --> 00:41:42.650
And we have to
translate the body here.

00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:48.090
Now, we have the
article in English,

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:51.440
we can use it for the Google
Cloud Natural Language

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:53.210
Processing API.

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:56.160
And this API does
three things for us.

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:59.420
First of all, we want to analyze
the sentiment of the article.

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:04.880
So the credit risk manager is
really interested in mainly

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:06.200
negative news.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:08.990
So a negative sentiment is more
important for a credit risk

00:42:08.990 --> 00:42:12.020
manager than a
positive sentiment.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.110
So we just analyze
the sentiment.

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:18.110
And here it says, OK, we
have a sentiment score which

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.990
is normally between minus
1, which is very negative,

00:42:20.990 --> 00:42:24.560
and 1, which is very
positive, of 0.26.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:27.000
It was quite positive.

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:28.730
And here, we have a magnitude.

00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:32.510
The magnitude is a
number that represents

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:36.930
the number of expressions of
emotions used in this text.

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:41.580
It's 3.3.

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:45.130
The second thing we do with
NLP is we analyze entities.

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.870
And this is really an
important part for us,

00:42:48.870 --> 00:42:51.150
because we want to
extract the entities.

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:55.290
And we want to know, which
entities is this article about?

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:57.930
So let's run it.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:01.860
Well, here you see it extracted
Ad van Gils as a person.

00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:06.280
And it also extracted PwC
Netherlands as an organization.

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:08.040
And here you see
why the Wikipedia

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:09.060
is so important for us.

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:13.450
Because what we had-- we
just searched for PwC.

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:15.630
And when we search
for PwC, we don't

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:20.010
know if the results are about
the PwC we are looking at.

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:23.560
But we know that when NLP
processed it and says,

00:43:23.560 --> 00:43:26.790
hey, this is the
Wikipedia of this company.

00:43:26.790 --> 00:43:30.166
And then, we know we have this
Wikipedia in on our database.

00:43:30.166 --> 00:43:31.290
Now, we can make the match.

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:34.940
This is really the company
we are interested in.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:36.250
And I saw this example.

00:43:36.250 --> 00:43:38.340
Let me go a bit up.

00:43:38.340 --> 00:43:42.630
Here it says, Waterpolo
Association PwC,

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:45.150
which is a completely
different entity.

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:46.940
We have a textual match.

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:52.380
But probably if we would
pass this through NLP,

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:54.600
it would not
recognize this entity

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:57.120
as being PricewaterhouseCoopers.

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:58.914
That's really important.

00:43:58.914 --> 00:44:00.580
What you also see,
here is the salience.

00:44:00.580 --> 00:44:04.060
And the salience is a
very important number.

00:44:04.060 --> 00:44:06.480
And it represents the
importance of an entity

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:08.550
within this article.

00:44:08.550 --> 00:44:10.650
So it's ordered by salience.

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.210
So it says Ad van Gils is
the most important entity.

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:15.660
And next to that, it's PwC.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:19.480
And here, we can make
a distinguish between,

00:44:19.480 --> 00:44:22.260
is this article really
about this entity,

00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:26.780
or did we just find it
somewhere in the article?

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:30.039
We also have a
sentiment per entity,

00:44:30.039 --> 00:44:32.330
where we have the same thing,
like score and magnitude.

00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:35.170
So if an article is negative,
we want to know, OK,

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:37.520
is it really negative
about that entity

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:40.010
or maybe about another entity?

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:41.750
We have a couple
of more entities.

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:45.220
We only have the proper
type entities in here,

00:44:45.220 --> 00:44:47.150
so not all entities found.

00:44:47.150 --> 00:44:50.527
And what you here see
is also type event.

00:44:50.527 --> 00:44:52.610
And this is really important
for clustering later.

00:44:52.610 --> 00:44:54.710
So it recognizes a
couple of events.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:58.900
So it was about Ad van Gils
being appointed as new CEO.

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:01.088
And we have appointment
as an event here.

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:08.700
The last thing NLP
does for us is analyze

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.470
the category of the article.

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:15.930
So Google configures a
couple of hundred categories,

00:45:15.930 --> 00:45:19.860
and they have this
NLP functionality

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:23.980
where it can predict in which
category the article is.

00:45:23.980 --> 00:45:27.090
So in the case that,
for example, PwC

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:29.640
would sponsor a sports
event, the article

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:31.920
will be pro-- the
article would probably

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:35.370
be classified as sports.

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:37.570
And that's a category
we're not interested in.

00:45:37.570 --> 00:45:41.580
So we only use white-listed
categories, like these--

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:43.860
Business and Industrial
or Company News.

00:45:43.860 --> 00:45:47.580
And here it says, it's
90% sure that this article

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:49.762
belongs to the category
Business and Industrial.

00:45:49.762 --> 00:45:51.970
So now, we can continue with
processing this article.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:58.090
But because these couple
of hundred categories

00:45:58.090 --> 00:46:01.270
are still quite
general and not based

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:05.530
or not specified for the
field of credit risk,

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:07.750
we defined a couple
of topics ourselves,

00:46:07.750 --> 00:46:12.910
and we trained a couple of
models that can classify

00:46:12.910 --> 00:46:14.860
the article for these topics.

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:21.040
So we trained more than these,
but a couple of models with

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:22.210
[INAUDIBLE] learn.

00:46:22.210 --> 00:46:25.120
And these are really
good at, for example,

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:27.810
predicting if an article
is about fraud or about

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:30.100
bankruptcy, the
topics that are really

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:32.320
important for that
credit risk manager.

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:35.300
So here, it says it's 11%
sure that it's about fraud,

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:39.190
8% sure about bankruptcy.

00:46:39.190 --> 00:46:45.460
Now we generate a summary, which
is a completely newly-generated

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:47.019
summary.

00:46:47.019 --> 00:46:48.560
So we can show this
in the dashboard.

00:46:48.560 --> 00:46:52.060
And after this, we can
remove the original body

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:54.620
and the translated body, because
we don't need it anymore.

00:46:54.620 --> 00:46:57.560
We extracted all the
information we need from it.

00:46:57.560 --> 00:47:03.000
And now, here comes the last
part, which is the clustering.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:04.990
So what we do with
clustering is we

00:47:04.990 --> 00:47:08.210
create one big matrix
where all the columns are

00:47:08.210 --> 00:47:11.010
entities we found and
all articles we found.

00:47:11.010 --> 00:47:12.920
So there are
hundreds of thousands

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:15.590
of columns in the matrix,
maybe even millions.

00:47:15.590 --> 00:47:16.740
I don't know.

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:19.250
And the rows are
individual articles.

00:47:19.250 --> 00:47:21.956
And here you see as a value,
the salience of that entity

00:47:21.956 --> 00:47:22.955
in the specific article.

00:47:25.770 --> 00:47:30.420
So for this example, we will
find a related article just as

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:33.390
an example, and we'll add
this to the matrix to show you

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:36.080
how we do this clustering.

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:41.980
So we found a related item
about this appointment.

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:43.610
And we add it to the matrix.

00:47:43.610 --> 00:47:47.870
So what you can see is a couple
of entities are added here.

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:50.410
So these entities are found
in the second article them

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:52.580
but not in the first article.

00:47:52.580 --> 00:47:56.500
And also, these entities
are not found in the first--

00:47:56.500 --> 00:47:59.620
are found in the first article
but not in the second article.

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:02.590
And we can also see
that still, Ad van Gils

00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:05.480
is the most important
entity in the first article

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:07.980
and the second article.

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:10.860
And what we can do
now is we calculate

00:48:10.860 --> 00:48:12.540
the cosine similarity,
which is just

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:15.415
a mathematical formula
which can calculate

00:48:15.415 --> 00:48:18.630
the distance between
two vectors, so

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:21.960
this vector and this vector.

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:25.840
And with this distance, we can
say, OK, these two articles

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:27.110
are about the same event.

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:29.280
So let's calculate it.

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:32.350
An here it says,
OK, I'm 82% sure--

00:48:32.350 --> 00:48:34.990
we have a 82% match that these--

00:48:34.990 --> 00:48:36.940
for these two vectors.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:38.530
And we can play
with the thresholds

00:48:38.530 --> 00:48:41.680
where we say, OK, if we
set the thresholds at 80%,

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:44.500
then these two articles
will be clustered together.

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:46.630
If we're set the
threshold at 85%,

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:49.900
they wouldn't be
clustered together.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:52.210
So that was it for the demo now.

00:48:52.210 --> 00:48:55.510
This, I think, cost
me like 15 minutes.

00:48:55.510 --> 00:48:58.540
I think on a work, it
will take like 15 seconds.

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:02.689
And we do it
100,000 times a day.

00:49:02.689 --> 00:49:04.480
And what really helped
us here was the fact

00:49:04.480 --> 00:49:07.900
that we used Kubernetes Engine
and Pub/Sub that we can scale.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:12.310
Now we have 250
counterparties in the tool,

00:49:12.310 --> 00:49:15.280
it will be increased
to maybe 10 times this.

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:18.070
And I'm confident
that we can scale

00:49:18.070 --> 00:49:22.330
to that amount of
counterparties.

00:49:22.330 --> 00:49:22.970
Thank you.

00:49:22.970 --> 00:49:24.120
I think back to Otto.

00:49:24.120 --> 00:49:28.521
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:34.364 --> 00:49:36.780
OTTO VERMEULEN: Thank you,
Peter, for this excellent demo.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.230
And I think now
we all understand

00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:41.110
why your hobby is Kubernetes.

00:49:41.110 --> 00:49:44.520
I think we've seen that we've
a lot of the components of GCP

00:49:44.520 --> 00:49:47.530
and also did quite
some coding ourselves.

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.150
So now, I think it's time
to wrap up this session.

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:54.260
And I want to give you
three key takeaways.

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:58.740
The first is the right
information at the right time.

00:49:58.740 --> 00:50:01.330
We have seen that an
international bank

00:50:01.330 --> 00:50:05.420
needs information from around
the globe at the right moments

00:50:05.420 --> 00:50:09.080
but also that information
is growing exponentially.

00:50:09.080 --> 00:50:12.430
So the key question was how
to get the right information

00:50:12.430 --> 00:50:15.790
at the right time at
the credit risk manager?

00:50:15.790 --> 00:50:18.150
And I think that with
this proof of concept,

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:21.250
we've shown that
you can do that.

00:50:21.250 --> 00:50:24.880
The second key takeaway is
Cloud and machine learning.

00:50:24.880 --> 00:50:27.970
Given what we've seen, in
the past three to five years

00:50:27.970 --> 00:50:31.380
ago, it would have been
inconceivable to make this.

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:34.030
But now, with the
advent of the Cloud

00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:37.360
and also all kinds of APIs
and algorithms for machine

00:50:37.360 --> 00:50:38.320
learning--

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:41.320
like translation, like
natural language processing--

00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:45.070
developing a system
like this is quite easy.

00:50:45.070 --> 00:50:49.700
And finally agile working
and fast experimentation--

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:53.350
we have demonstrated that
with the right tooling

00:50:53.350 --> 00:50:56.770
and the right team, you can
build a proof of concept

00:50:56.770 --> 00:50:59.620
from scratch in just 12 weeks.

00:50:59.620 --> 00:51:01.920
And you can do that as
well and start tomorrow.

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:03.170
Thank you for your time.

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:04.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:11.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

